    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d657">

                <pb facs="1078" n="1062" sameAs="frus:frus1951v07p1/pg1062"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/10–2551: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gifford</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-10-25T19:00:00+00:00">October 25, 1951—7
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2029. Embtel 1968, October 22.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d657-f1"/> Following
                    interview with Vyshinsky Oct 23, Brit Amb Moscow reported to FonOff that
                    although Vyshinsky promised to consider possibility USSR again using good
                    offices with North Koreans to urge continuance transmission letters between Brit
                    POWs and their relatives in UK, his attitude unsatisfactory on question exerting
                    influence re freeing Holt<anchor n="2" xml:id="d657-f2"/> and others his party.
                    Vyshinsky implied again that it embarrassing to USSR to intervene. When
                    Gascoigne pleaded length and abnormal character detention, Vyshinsky said there
                    different interpretations of requirements international law re civilian
                    internees. After Gascoigne noted Holt and at least one companion had diplomatic
                    status, Vyshinsky merely insisted best way obtaining their freedom was to
                    arrange cease-fire, for <pb facs="1079" n="1063" xml:id="pg1063"/> which USSR
                    anxious. He also agreed with Gascoigne’s assertion one major consideration in
                    favor cease-fire is that this wld be important step in easing international
                    tension. Gascoigne in his report seemed impressed by this, remarking that “on
                    face of it, USSR seems anxious for cease-fire”.</p>
                <p>Dept pass Moscow; sent Dept 2029, rptd info Moscow 35.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gifford</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d657-f1" n="1" xml:id="d657-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed. It reported that the Foreign Office, influenced by the views of
                    Messrs. Bohlen and Rusk as expressed to British Embassy officials in Washington
                    and transmitted to London, had wired the new British Ambassador in Moscow, Sir
                    Alvary Gascoigne, to refrain from making representations on Korea during his
                    call on Soviet Foreign Minister Vyshinsky (795.00/10–2251).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d657-f2" n="2" xml:id="d657-f2-content">
                <p>Captain Vyvyan Holt, British Minister in the Republic of Korea, had been interned
                    by the North Koreans following the outbreak of fighting in 1950. He was not
                    released until 1953.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
